today I'm going to talk about invisible  problems every society is plagued by  problems that are particularly hard to  solve precisely because nobody can see  them so to give you an example the  ancient Greeks were remarkable  innovators both in politics and in the  sciences but even the most enlightened  thinkers of the day thought that women  were inferior to men and that slavery  was actually good for slaves  so in our day were confronted with a  host of difficult problems poverty  violence environmental and financial  catastrophes I believe these problems  are caused in part by a deeper form of  inequality than the one that plagued the  Greeks and until we can all see this  root cause clearly we're going to  struggle to solve our more serious  problems so the root cause is very  simple so we underestimate ourselves I  first saw this through my own experience  I'm a mathematician and playwright but  when I was growing up I had deep doubts  about my abilities these were confirmed  when I almost failed my first calculus  course at university fortunately I was  saved by the bell curve that brought my  mark up to a c-minus but unfortunately I  also got the lowest mark in my creative  writing class so when I graduated from  university I didn't think there was too  much I could do it took me a long time  to develop the confidence to go into  these fields and fifteen years ago I  started a charity in my apartment called  jump math because I didn't want kids to  struggle in math the way I had jump  produces materials for students training  and resources for teachers and our  student books are used by a hundred and  fifty thousand children now in Canada in  the u.s. we also participate in rigorous  research including two randomized  controlled studies so to give you an  idea of the extent to which we  underestimate ourselves I'd like to  start with a case study  at the beginning of grade five honto  public school teacher mary-jane Moreau  tested her children on a test called  automa a standardized test of  mathematical ability and the average  mark in the class was in the 54th  percentile there was quite a range of  marks the lowest mark was in the tenth  percentile and the highest mark was in  the 75th percentile so I've seen this  same range in all of the schools I  visited this would represent about a  three grade level difference between the  top students and the bottom students as  early as grade five so Mary Jane tried  using the jump lesson plans for a year  and she retested her kids at the  beginning of grade six and that's what  the bell curve looked like the lowest  mark was in the 95th percentile and the  average was in the 98th and at the end  of grade six all but one of the students  wrote the Pythagoras math competition  and 14 of the 17 got Awards of  distinction and the other three came  close now this is just a case study but  in a much larger randomized controlled  study students in jump classes  progressed at twice the rate of the  control group and there's a growing body  of evidence in cognitive science that  virtually every child is born with the  capacity to do well in any subject so  the question is why don't we see these  kind of things in our society there are  many reasons but I'm going to focus on  four first until recently there haven't  been many rigorous studies in math  education some people have argued that  kids should be allowed to discover  things on their own and others that they  should be taught more explicitly a new  body of research in cognitive science  suggests that a balanced approach called  guided discovery produces the best  results so in guided discovery children  are given opportunities to discover  things for themselves but the teacher  carefully guides their learning by  providing lots of feedback practice  review by breaking problems into more  manageable steps so if you're interested  in this research you can find a summary  in an article  for Scientific American mind at jump  math org now it's very important that we  pay attention to this research in  education because we're often swayed by  arguments that aren't founded in very  solid data or evidence so for instance  some people argue that because we have  the internet kids don't learn to need to  learn facts now they need to learn how  to find and analyze facts now there's  some truth to this but research suggests  that kids who don't know basic facts  like multiplication tables often  struggle to become good problem solvers  their memories are constantly  overwhelmed trying to remember the facts  and they have trouble making predictions  and seeing patterns so recently I tried  a lesson on multiplication with a grade  three class using some charts I'm going  to show you I was very surprised the  kids managed to work through seven of  these charts in one lesson and  afterwards I found out that some of them  were only in grade two so I hope you'll  do as well as he's great to students so  I asked the kids to write the first  three multiples of three in a row like  this and then put the rest underneath  and add zeros the kids very quickly saw  a pattern in the tens digits and also in  the ones digits now they saw lots of  other patterns but I don't have much  time today so maybe you can think about  those on your own maybe when you go home  or having trouble sleeping so after a  little practice the kids were able to  write out these charts from memory using  the patterns they found and then I told  them to think of the touch pad on a  phone if you know the position of the  numbers on in the chart then you know  how to multiply by three so for instance  the number 18 is in position six and six  times three is 18 and the number 24 is  in position eight and eight times three  is twenty-four  now I'm a mathematician but I didn't  realize until a few months ago that if  you put other odd numbers like seven and  nine or the multiples in rows of three  like this you see similar patterns and  if you put the even the multiples of  even numbers in rows  five you see lots more patterns now if  this was a grade three class some of you  might already know your times tables I  know there's a lot of physicists here so  I expect that's the case and some of you  might be very good at seeing patterns so  how can I keep you engaged in the lesson  well the beautiful thing about  mathematics is that if you if you know  the math you can always create bonus  questions that don't introduce new  skills or topics but that look harder so  a good bonus question here would be can  you see any patterns that are common to  all of the charts if you look at the  tens digit for instance you'll notice in  the chart four to the tens digit  increases by one in the chart for four  it increases by two in the chart for six  it increases by three and so on  the tens digit always goes up by half of  what you're multiplying by now the  really exciting thing and I've seen this  with thousands of students is that when  students who are initially slower see  that the extra work is possible that  they can do it they start speeding up  dramatically so they can get their bonus  questions so we don't have to hold back  faster students to close the gap  Maryjane's results showed this very  clearly now these are fairly simple  charts but you can teach much more  complicated things using guided  discovery even problem solving but to  teach this way you have to know the math  well so this coming to a second problem  in education teachers are my absolute  heroes but many teachers will admit that  they don't know the math and they don't  love teaching it at a teachers  convention I asked a large group of  teachers why when you divide seven by  two-thirds you can flip and multiply and  someone yelled out because you get the  right answer so that's true to give  teachers the professional development  they deserve in mathematics can be very  expensive so a jump we decided to take  the expertise of a team of  mathematicians and expert teachers and  put that knowledge into clear  comprehensive lesson plans so that  teachers can learn the math as they  teach it if you register at jump math  org you'll find lessons for Kanda  the US common core even even a video  that explains why you flip and multiply  now a third a third reason we struggle  to teach math well is because we think  it's an inherently hard and boring  subject now you wouldn't think that  learning to read numbers is very  exciting but listen to these grade two  students three three hundred fifteen  thousand four hundred unbelievable okay  like I really wasn't planning good so I  think we have to go up the billions  billions all right this is the last one  okay anything to give to millions yeah  who thinks they can do billions yeah you  think you could do it I I think you  could do it what do you think you want  to try it so you did it that's fantastic  okay I got you guys I can't really visit  any harder I could go to trillions yeah  so I've seen that same kind of  excitement at every grade level I've  seen kids cheer for math and I've seen  them beg to stay for recess I even broke  up a fight once by telling the bully  that if you didn't apologize I wouldn't  give him this bonus question  and he apologized and this brings me to  my last and only radical point we're  confronted with a circular problem in  education we teach math and other  subjects in a way that causes many  students to struggle and this reinforces  our belief that only a few are born with  ability but what if this belief itself  was the main cause of our problem as  early as grade 1 children start  comparing themselves to their peers and  deciding who's smart and who's dumb in  subjects like math once children have  decided they're not talented their  brains stop working efficiently they  stop persevering paying attention they  even develop anxieties and behavioral  problems but teachers like Mary Jane  have shown that we don't have to teach  this way once you've seen how much  children can learn and how much they  love learning you have to admit that  we're living in an age of intellectual  poverty if children are born with  anything it's a sense of the invisible  beauty of the world they love seeing  patterns making connections solving  puzzles as you can see from this lesson  of children learning about of all things  binary codes but the majority will lose  this sense of wonder and curiosity  before they grow up because as a society  we expect so little of ourselves every  child has a right to fulfill their  intellectual potential just as they have  a right to develop healthy bodies and we  don't have to wait until we've invented  some miraculous new technology or  discovered a new method of teaching to  guarantee this right we could accomplish  more now as a society than all the  towering geniuses of the past 4 if we  were merely to educate our children  using methods based on rigorous  evidence we would be the last generation  whose promise is lost  thank you

